---
import "../styles/global.css";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Methodology · OSS Maintainer Insights</title>
    <link rel="icon" href={`${base}favicon.svg`} type="image/svg+xml" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <a class="skip-link" href="#main">
      Skip to content
    </a>
    <header class="topbar wrap">
      <a class="brand" href={base}>
        <span class="brand-icon" aria-hidden="true">
          ◈
        </span>
        <span>
          OSS Maintainer <strong>Insights</strong>
        </span>
      </a>
      <nav aria-label="Main navigation">
        <a href={base}>Explore repositories</a>
      </nav>
    </header>
    <main id="main" class="wrap methodology">
      <p class="eyebrow">METHODOLOGY 1.1.0</p>
      <h1>
        Read the numbers
        <br />
        <em>with their context.</em>
      </h1>
      <p class="lead">
        These metrics describe observed review activity. They do not identify an
        authoritative maintainer roster or assign a project health score.
      </p>
      <section class="panel">
        <h2>A bounded live sample</h2>
        <p>
          Each query selects up to eight of the newest eligible PRs created in
          the preceding 90 days from GitHub's first 30-record page, sorted by
          creation time. Currently draft PRs, bot authors, and deleted authors
          are excluded. The query does not replace unreviewed PRs with more
          active ones. This is a sample, not a full 90-day repository census.
        </p>
        <p>
          Queries make at most 20 API requests, including retries, and collect
          all review streams before using the remaining budget for comments.
          Pagination, rate limits, network failures, and cancellation can leave
          partial coverage. Results show the observation time and the covered
          and eligible counts for each metric.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="panel">
        <h2>Saved catalog samples</h2>
        <p>
          Saved catalog data selects up to 18 PRs across the preceding 90 days:
          up to six from each disjoint 30-day creation band. Each band inspects
          the newest 30 search candidates, then confirms current PR details.
          Human-authored, non-draft PRs are selected by creation time, without
          requiring reviews or responses. Empty bands stay empty. The selected
          PR age range is measured at collection time and shown with the sample.
        </p>
        <p>
          This bounded selection favors newer PRs within each band. It is not a
          random sample, a full 90-day census, or complete historical review
          activity. A complete collection means all selected event streams
          finished; it does not mean every PR in the repository was collected.
          Missing headline metrics describe this sample, not repository health.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="panel">
        <h2>Review participation</h2>
        <p>
          One review unit is one reviewer–PR pair with at least one submitted
          approved, changes requested, or commented review, or an active human
          /lgtm or /approve command. Repeated reviews and commands by the same
          GitHub user ID on a PR count once. Pending and dismissed reviews,
          bots, deleted actors, self reviews, and events outside
          creation-to-observation time are excluded. A PR contributes only when
          its review and both comment streams are complete.
        </p>
        <p>
          Commands are standalone lines in comments or qualifying review bodies.
          Quoted examples and code blocks are ignored. Cancel and remove
          commands withdraw that person's corresponding signal; another active
          command or a GitHub review can still retain the pair. Source counts
          distinguish GitHub reviews and Prow commands and can overlap. These
          are observed review intentions; OWNERS authorization and Prow label
          state are not inferred. Methodology 1.1.0 applies this convention
          across repositories.
        </p>
        <p>
          Concentration 80 is the smallest number of reviewers whose units reach
          at least 80% of all units. Its headline requires at least five PRs
          with complete review coverage and at least one review unit. The
          distribution and raw covered counts remain visible with smaller
          samples.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="panel">
        <h2>First human response</h2>
        <p>
          The earliest qualifying review, issue comment, or inline comment from
          a human other than the PR author is a response. All three event
          streams must be complete. Uncovered PRs are not labeled unanswered. A
          response exactly at creation has a zero duration; an unanswered PR has
          no duration.
        </p>
        <p>
          The median is shown only with at least five responded PRs. For an even
          count, it averages the two middle durations. Responded, unanswered,
          and fully covered counts are displayed separately.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="panel">
        <h2>Freshness, privacy, and limits</h2>
        <p>
          Complete normalized samples can be cached in this browser for one
          hour, keyed by repository ID and the schema, methodology, and
          query-policy versions. Refresh bypasses the cache. Failed or partial
          refreshes cannot overwrite a complete cache entry. Clear cache removes
          the stored sample.
        </p>
        <p>
          Browser queries are unauthenticated and contain no credentials.
          Comment bodies and PR titles are not retained in the analysis or
          cache. Unknown automation and changes to current review states are
          limitations. Historical trends and a reviewed repository catalog are
          planned separately.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="https://github.com/cpanato/oss-maintainer-insights/blob/main/docs/methodology.md">
            Read the full methodology and versioning rules ↗
          </a>
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>
    <footer class="wrap">
      <p>Observed activity. Transparent coverage.</p>
      <p>No health scores · No maintainer roster inferred</p>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
